But Dom, can you
explain the end result?

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:26.240
What do you see here?

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.040
What is Data Studio give you--

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.384
what is the actual product
that you have finally

00:10:30.384 --> 00:10:31.300
built at the very end?

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:32.970
And then maybe we can
move backwards from there.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.719
DOM ELLIOTT: Sure.

00:10:33.719 --> 00:10:36.140
I would sort of
describe it as a--

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:38.990
if you were traditionalist,
like a living version

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:42.850
of presentation, and
each slide is essentially

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.922
a report on a different
marketing channel that we use,

00:10:45.922 --> 00:10:46.880
and it's really visual.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:47.790
It's in Data Studio.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:51.260
It's got graphs and
stats, and each graph

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.420
has trends versus
previous months.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:57.140
We sort of slice data based on
different things like country,

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:00.230
or engagement by
different dimensions

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:01.550
depending on what the data is.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:06.110
We have about 11 different
pages in this report, one

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:08.270
for each of our main
marketing channels,

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:11.990
going from web, to app, to
email, to ads, to video,

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.404
to blog posts, and then social,
and then events as well.

00:11:15.404 --> 00:11:16.820
MELANIE WARRICK:
And is part of it

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:18.680
used to help decide
where to spend

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:21.140
more efforts towards
different marketing channels?

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:22.765
What are some of the
decisions that you

00:11:22.765 --> 00:11:23.840
like to make off of this?

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.423
DOM ELLIOTT: At a
high level, it helps

00:11:25.423 --> 00:11:27.720
us understand the
health of each channel.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.420
Are these channels growing?

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.460
Are there particular
countries which

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:33.590
are very engaged,
or not very engaged,

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:36.200
where we should dial up
or dial down our efforts?

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.650
For specific channels,
we can see if--

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:41.690
for the app data we
pull in from Firebase,

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:43.790
for example, when we
release an update,

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:47.150
is that resulting in a sudden
increase or a sudden drop

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:50.460
in engagement we can see
those kind of things,

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.510
and then it helps us a lot with
the various pieces of content

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:55.280
we put out on channels, as well.

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:58.130
It helps us with optimization
and planning going for us.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:58.880
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.463
DOM ELLIOTT: Another
use of the report

00:12:00.463 --> 00:12:02.190
is internal reporting,
essentially,

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:03.950
so we can tell
various stakeholders

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:06.050
in all the other teams,
the product team, business

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:08.780
development team, our
various VPs, when they just

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:11.630
want to dive in and just get
a snapshot of what's going on,

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:14.150
see how engaged and
developed the community is

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:17.030
in various places, and they
can do that pretty easily now.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:19.490
When those kind of fire
drills came in in the past,

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:22.772
we would all have to run
along and pull all that data

00:12:22.772 --> 00:12:24.980
on the fly, but now we just
have it all in one place.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:25.940
MARK MANDEL: Lovely.

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:28.340
So Stewart, I'm going
to come to you now.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:30.320
We have the GCP podcast.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.390
You mentioned
previously that this

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:34.970
is a lot of serverless stuff,
sort of unmanaged as much as

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:35.482
possible.

00:12:35.482 --> 00:12:37.940
What products did you actually
use to build this whole data

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:38.640
pipeline?

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.973
STEWART BRYSON: Yeah, we went
through several iterations

00:12:40.973 --> 00:12:44.120
and the total project length
was about three months.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.669
We took a look at, really, the
landscape of all the products

00:12:46.669 --> 00:12:47.210
we could use.

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:49.520
We initially considered
data flow, but really

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.970
for these, for instance
Twitter has a streaming API

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:55.490
and JavaScript, really
fit the bill well,

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:59.570
so at the very heart
of this is App Engine.

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:02.210
App Engine is the
application is pulling

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.060
all the different
social media APIs,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.250
so App Engine grabs that
stuff, and initially

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:10.400
our first iteration was
just App Engine to BigQuery.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.970
Some great node packages for
writing directly to BigQuery

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:18.110
from JavaScript, and that
was the initial iteration,

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:20.030
but then we started
to rethink it

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:22.550
as we added additional
social media channels.

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:24.860
So we went after Twitter,
and then LinkedIn,

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:28.250
and then we looked at
Medium, so as we architected

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:30.230
this in a very iterative
way, we started

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:32.240
introducing some other aspects.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:34.880
So Pub/Sub became
valuable for us

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.205
because initially
we're streaming data--

00:13:37.205 --> 00:13:39.890
we're using the streaming API
to pull data from Twitter,

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:42.200
but that doesn't give
us likes and retweets.

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:44.030
The streaming API
doesn't give that,

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:47.730
and that's really what Dom's
team was super interested in,

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:51.080
so we had to use the
batch API against Twitter

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:53.690
to pull all those
retweets and likes,

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:56.090
and Pub/Sub subscriptions
made it very easy

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.890
for us to manage going at the
data from multiple avenues,

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:04.550
and then again, we
started to, as we ingested

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:07.130
more and more social
media sources,

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:09.350
we introduced Cloud
Functions, and Cloud Functions

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:12.460
allowed us to be a little
bit more event driven

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:16.220
in how these different sort
of social media messages

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:17.340
are processed.

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:19.910
So the final
architecture was, really,

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:23.370
App Engine, pulling data from
all of these different places,

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:27.230
ingesting into Pub/Sub, and then
having Cloud Functions react

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:28.700
to that ingestion.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.940
And part of it was just using
the Natural Language API

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:34.130
that Google has
available, so we were also

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:36.710
tracking sentiment analysis,
which wasn't really

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:38.540
initially one of
the requirements,

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:41.330
but it was so easy to do
that we started doing it,

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:43.760
and I think Dom and his
team have really gotten

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:45.790
good value out of that aspect.

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:48.050
Not just seeing how
many retreats and likes,

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:53.300
but also measuring the sentiment
of those different social media

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:54.420
messages.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:56.210
MELANIE WARRICK: So that's great
that you're using sentiment.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.251
What are some of the ways
that you're applying it

00:14:58.251 --> 00:15:00.890
in terms of utilizing it for
decisions that you're making,

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:02.960
or how are you
looking at sentiment?

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:05.090
DOM ELLIOTT: On Twitter
and LinkedIn in particular,

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:08.780
we're seeing how sentiments
tracking over time.

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:13.370
We're looking out for any dips
or any particular increases

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:17.210
and seeing what are the
popular posts around that time,

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:19.010
and because of the
data we're showing,

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:22.440
we can then go and
see which handles

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:25.670
are getting a lot of
engagement around those tweets,

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:28.070
and then maybe we can, if
it's something a little bit

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:30.560
negative, we can
see if we can help.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:33.350
We can point people to
our support resources,

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.160
or to our operations
team and if there's

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.160
something we can help
with, if there's something

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.040
we should escalate
on Google Play.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.600
Or if it's positive,
maybe we can retweet it,

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:43.730
or look at what the
developer audience is sort

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:45.034
of enjoying and engaging with.

00:15:45.034 --> 00:15:46.700
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice,
and then Stewart,

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:48.110
that was a really
great overview in terms

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:50.310
of that type of products
that you guys are using.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.220
What were some of the challenges
you were up against when

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:53.360
you were building this out?

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:55.401
STEWART BRYSON: Well, time
was one of the things,

00:15:55.401 --> 00:15:58.340
so we had to build this in
a reasonably quick amount

00:15:58.340 --> 00:15:58.980
of time.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:02.390
Three month project for
this many data sources was--

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:06.140
I won't say daunting, but
an on prem implementation

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:08.130
would have taken
much, much longer.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:10.220
I think the idea that
we were just able to--

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:13.320
there's no provisioning,
just get up and get going--

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:14.390
was an important part.

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:17.210
I think also in a
project like this,

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:19.740
we often focus a lot
on the data movement

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:23.420
and how the data is ingested
and ultimately finds

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:27.770
its home in BigQuery,
but sometimes Dom

00:16:27.770 --> 00:16:31.400
had some very specific
visualization requirements.

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:34.410
He knew what he wanted
it to look like.

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:36.440
He wanted to be
able to present this

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.690
to management and to
the rest of his team,

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:41.270
so really, Data Studio
shines in that way.

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:45.290
It's based on Google Drive,
so we can make changes

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:48.636
to Data Studio reports and
dashboards as Dom was watching,

00:16:48.636 --> 00:16:50.510
and he could even dip
his hand in there a bit

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:52.730
and say, no, I'm
thinking more like this,

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:56.150
and we could see those
changes at the same time.

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.250
So it's really unusual
for an analytics tool

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:01.080
to have that kind of
interactive capabilities,

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.860
and I think the
iterative process

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:07.099
of trying out some
visualizations,

00:17:07.099 --> 00:17:10.579
seeing if it sticks, seeing if
it fits what Dom has in mind

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:14.790
for trying to report on this
stuff, was really valuable.

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:17.000
So I think, from a
high level perspective,

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.030
it's just the challenge of
trying to get this many data

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:23.960
sources visualized in exactly
the way he wanted it was really

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:26.480
the biggest challenge,
and I can't imagine.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:31.370
There is no analog equivalent to
trying to do that without Cloud

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:32.290
architecture.

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:34.790
MARK MANDEL: Stewart, I got an
interesting question for you.

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:36.440
So, you mentioned
a little while ago

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:38.982
that you're using both App
Engine and Cloud Functions.

00:17:38.982 --> 00:17:40.940
I know something we've
talked about in the past

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:44.270
is, where to apply, what
type of compute resource

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:45.426
to what sort of workload.

00:17:45.426 --> 00:17:48.050
I'm curious to know how you made
the decision about what things

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:49.466
were going to end
up on App Engine

00:17:49.466 --> 00:17:51.830
and what things would end up
on Cloud Functions and why.

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:54.680
STEWART BRYSON: So initially,
everything was in App Engine,

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:57.470
so that was just the
easiest way to do it.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:00.170
We were pulling, and
in the App Engine code

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:02.810
we were pulling and
writing the BigQuery.

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:06.470
It was as we started ingesting
multiple data sources that we

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:10.250
realized we needed to respond to
this in more of an event driven

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:11.120
manner.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:14.360
For instance, the Twitter
example, the streaming API

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:16.130
versus the batch API.

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:19.220
It would be difficult to
write that sort of two

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:23.630
headed process in App Engine,
so one of the easiest decisions

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:27.230
was to start ingesting
that stuff in the Pub/Sub

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:30.710
so that you could have
one Cloud Function that's

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:34.520
responding to the events as
they're going into Pub/Sub,

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.550
but then App Engine was able
to continue to do the streaming

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:41.600
side of it, so it
was the approach

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:45.470
to trying to both pull
from a streaming API,

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:49.520
and also periodically
process using the batch

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:52.640
API, some of those
same datasets.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.380
We had to work through
what we thought

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:55.980
was the life of a tweet.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:57.830
There's a lot of
thoughts on that,

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:00.650
but I think we decided
on seven days, right Dom?

00:19:00.650 --> 00:19:02.690
So for seven days
we would continually

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:05.420
look at-- a tweet came
through the streaming API,

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:08.420
and it became very easy
with the Cloud Function

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:10.700
to respond to those
streaming tweets

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.490
and periodically go
query the batch API

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:16.291
to find out if there's been
any retweets, likes, et cetera.

00:19:16.291 --> 00:19:18.790
MELANIE WARRICK: Do you have a
rough gauge for how much data

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:22.040
you were processing per
day, per minute, per hour?

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:24.590
STEWART BRYSON: It's really
not a lot when you look at it.

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:27.920
We've got some targeted
hashtags, targeted handles

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:30.580
for all these different
social media avenues,

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:33.740
and it's the other reason why
we shied away from Dataflow.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:38.090
Dataflow can process huge
amounts of data at scale,

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:41.450
but it would have required us
to dip into Java or Python,

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:44.870
so the fact that App Engine
and Cloud Functions gave us

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:47.150
the ability to do a lot
of this in JavaScript,

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:50.960
and that's the best language
to be working with some

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:52.716
of the social media APIs.

00:19:52.716 --> 00:19:54.590
MELANIE WARRICK: The
question I wanted to ask

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:58.070
is that since clearly you've
been using GCP products,

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:00.825
is this common that you see
these types of combinations?

00:20:00.825 --> 00:20:02.450
What are some things
that you typically

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:04.866
see, or the type of products
that you need to put together

00:20:04.866 --> 00:20:06.439
for your clients?

00:20:06.439 --> 00:20:08.730
STEWART BRYSON: Yeah, really
when you start looking at,

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:12.240
is the data sitting
behind a REST API.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:13.880
So in the past when
you-- especially

00:20:13.880 --> 00:20:16.220
with on prem software,
it's everything's

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:18.040
so relational in nature, right?

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:19.930
Your source systems
are relational based.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:23.390
Your target data warehouses
are relational based.

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:26.690
I think when you look
at where does GCP really

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:29.930
shine for analytics
workloads, and obviously it

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:32.830
can handle all of those
use cases as well,

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:36.020
but it's the API driven approach
that analytics is really

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:38.120
moving to today, and
that is that data

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:39.560
is coming from a lot of places.

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:41.390
It's not a single
source system that

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:43.490
has all of your information.

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.470
As more and more
companies our size,

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:48.920
and even companies the size
of Google, a lot of their data

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:52.280
is sitting in a lot of
disparate places, and the fact

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.340
that we can write these
event driven workflows that

00:20:55.340 --> 00:20:58.730
can go grab this data
from behind a REST API,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:02.120
process it in a
meaningful way, and also

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:05.540
the iterative approach for
which we were able to take

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:10.650
social media sources, architect
them from start to finish,

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:14.150
land them in BigQuery, and
start building reports in just

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.180
a few days.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.690
So the value that
it really brings--

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:20.270
and I think Dom could speak
to this as well-- the value

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:22.610
that it really brings
is how quickly we were

00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:25.370
able to hit the ground
running, how quickly we were

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:29.510
able to prototype a
particular digital channel,

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:33.350
develop a dashboard or in
a report in Data Studio,

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:36.200
get feedback, loop
back around and start

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:39.860
that iterative process again,
and we were constantly in flow

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:43.130
with building and delivering
additional data sets,

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:46.850
and just the interactive
and collaborative approach

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:50.900
that Data Studio takes is really
unlike any other visualization

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:52.730
tool that we've worked with.

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:56.300
The ability to be able to
just, everyone collaborate

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:58.670
on a Data Studio
report in the same way

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:01.500
you would with Google
Docs or Google Sheets,

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:05.540
it was really valuable
from that whole analytics

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:08.750
process of trying to get
exactly the right visualization

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:10.760
that Dom's team was
looking for, and that

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:14.110
took several iterations
to get it just right,

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:17.362
and we were able to do that
really easily in Data Studio.

00:22:17.362 --> 00:22:19.320
MARK MANDEL: Dom, how is
the experience for you

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:21.890
of this being developed
as you being the end user?

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:24.110
DOM ELLIOTT: I agree with
most of what Stewart said.

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:25.880
It was great, actually.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:28.730
I was constantly surprised
at what was possible, really.

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:32.870
It was really very helpful
doing everything iteratively

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:36.970
because I had rough ideas
at the very beginning,

00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:39.780
but I didn't fully know what
was possible in these products,

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:42.370
and so I'd sit down
with Stewart and Phil

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:45.170
and we'd talk through
various ideas,

00:22:45.170 --> 00:22:47.060
and they would let me
know what's possible,

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:49.160
and then they could go
away and just put something

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:51.650
together straightaway really
quickly, something scrappy,

00:22:51.650 --> 00:22:52.790
and we'd have a look
at that, and then

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:54.030
we could keep iterating.

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:56.810
So it worked very well, and
then yeah, as you mentioned,

00:22:56.810 --> 00:22:58.160
Data Studio has--

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:01.650
the front end UI is like using
Google Docs or something,

00:23:01.650 --> 00:23:06.100
so I could jump in and toggle,
test out changing little things

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:08.429
myself without actually
going into the code.

00:23:08.429 --> 00:23:10.220
MARK MANDEL: And Dom
I'm also wondering now

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:12.200
that you have this
tool, what's been

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.060
the most interesting
or surprising metric

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:15.410
that you've been able
to pull out of it

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:16.400
that you wouldn't
have necessarily would

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:17.566
have been able to do before?

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:22.762
DOM ELLIOTT: The data is
buried in different places.

00:23:22.762 --> 00:23:24.720
Well, we didn't have the
sentiment data at all,

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:26.330
so that is really interesting.

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:28.580
Some of the things
I like the most

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:31.220
are diving into
different countries

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:33.560
and just seeing the
engagement that we

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:34.970
get in different countries.

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:37.730
Android is exploding
at the moment in--

00:23:37.730 --> 00:23:40.540
we call them next for
the end user markets,

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:44.030
or growing markets like India,
and Vietnam, and Indonesia,

00:23:44.030 --> 00:23:45.830
and I'm able to see
in this dashboard,

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:47.870
as well, going through
the various channels,

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:50.880
we have huge engagement
in those regions.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:52.770
We can see specific
countries who are really,

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:55.280
really engaged on our
channel and consuming

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:58.070
all of this content, and that's
been really great, so we can

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:00.770
then see, OK, they're reading--

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:02.774
or this-- this
country, say Vietnam,

00:24:02.774 --> 00:24:04.940
is really, really engaged
in reading all our content

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:08.360
in English, and so we can
do a better job there,

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:11.240
and we can go and localize the
content because the developer

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:14.450
audience is there, and produce
more content in Vietnamese

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:19.424
for them, so those kind of loops
have been really satisfying.

00:24:19.424 --> 00:24:21.840
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, I know
we're getting close to time,

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.170
but I wanted to ask
you-- and both of you--

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:26.174
what are some key lessons
learned, takeaways,

00:24:26.174 --> 00:24:28.590
that you haven't already talked
about that you'd recommend

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:30.714
to people who are considering
this type of project,

00:24:30.714 --> 00:24:32.477
or something along these lines?

00:24:32.477 --> 00:24:34.060
STEWART BRYSON: I
think, be iterative.

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:37.340
I've mentioned that
before, but jump in.

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:40.100
One of the best things about
the Google Cloud Platform

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:43.730
is that you can get
started on day one.

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:46.160
For instance, Data Studio
has direct connectors

00:24:46.160 --> 00:24:48.830
to a lot of these
sources, and although we

00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:51.470
knew that we wanted to
land the data in BigQuery

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:55.817
for posterity's sake, we were
able to prototype immediately

00:24:55.817 --> 00:24:58.400
with the built in connectors to
things like YouTube and Google

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:02.530
Analytics, and start that whole
process of trying to figure out

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:06.510
exactly how Dom and his team
wanted to see that data.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:10.100
So while we had one member of
the team with direct connectors

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:12.410
from Data Studio
working with Dom's team,

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:15.740
really on day one
prototyping some of the ways

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:18.364
that he was thinking about
visualizing this data.

00:25:18.364 --> 00:25:20.030
We had other members
of the team working

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:23.990
on capturing that data using
things like App Engine,

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:26.810
writing that data to
BigQuery, figuring out

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:31.400
how we wanted to
store it in BigQuery,

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:35.389
and it was a complete iterative
process, so once the data--

00:25:35.389 --> 00:25:37.430
it took us a little time
to get the data transfer

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:39.080
service running.

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:40.970
Once that data was
landing in BigQuery,

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:45.710
we slowly refactored Data
Studio reports and dashboards

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:47.860
to go after the BigQuery data.

00:25:47.860 --> 00:25:51.020
In some instances we
continued to report

00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:54.395
against the native connectors,
so we have dashboards

00:25:54.395 --> 00:25:59.780
that blend content from the
native connectors with the data

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:04.250
from BigQuery, and that
flexibility was really

00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:05.480
eye opening, I think.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:08.060
In a lot of analytics projects
you spend so much time

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:09.860
trying to land and
ingest the data

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:11.600
and get the data
just right, and then

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:14.750
the analytics starts,
and quite often

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:17.570
that piece is kind of brushed
over because you're running out

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:20.209
of time, and maybe you
don't get exactly what

00:26:20.209 --> 00:26:22.250
the end user was looking
for from a visualization

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:23.480
perspective.

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:26.510
We were able to start
the visualization

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:30.350
process immediately using
the native connectors,

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:32.850
and then just slowly
refactor over time,

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:37.190
so I think if I were to take
one major lesson from building

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:38.630
analytics, especially
a Greenfield

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:41.840
implementation in the
Google Cloud platform,

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:43.010
is just be iterative.

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:46.100
Roll up your sleeves, get
started, and also don't be

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:48.230
afraid to write a little code.

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:51.620
So with APIs, it's going to
be difficult to find just

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:56.150
the right tooling to go after
something that's API driven,

00:26:56.150 --> 00:27:00.200
so you've got to not
be afraid to use tools

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:03.230
like Cloud Functions, use
a tool like App Engine

00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:07.801
and Pub/Sub to build the right
event driven architecture.

00:27:07.801 --> 00:27:08.550
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:27:08.550 --> 00:27:09.529
How about you, Dom?

00:27:09.529 --> 00:27:11.820
DOM ELLIOTT: In general I
was just pleasantly surprised

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:13.410
at what's possible.

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:15.560
I think the kind of
small amount of work

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:20.030
up front is really worth it
to save time in the long run,

00:27:20.030 --> 00:27:21.770
and you can--

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:24.200
then these reports
are very flexible

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:26.820
and can help you save time
and do your job better,

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:29.000
and on Data Studio
in particular,

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:30.770
I love a bunch of
their visualizations

00:27:30.770 --> 00:27:33.187
we're using now, which we
just weren't using before.

00:27:33.187 --> 00:27:35.270
Well, we weren't using
bubble charts at all before

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:39.090
and now they're one of my
favorite visualizations,

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:42.470
and we've got heat maps, and
geographic maps, and just

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:46.670
a bunch more
interesting, insightful

00:27:46.670 --> 00:27:49.430
visualizations that
we can start to draw

00:27:49.430 --> 00:27:50.840
interesting conclusions from.

00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:51.360
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:53.720
MELANIE WARRICK: It's expanding
your knowledge on the system.

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:54.740
DOM ELLIOTT: Yeah, exactly.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:55.460
STEWART BRYSON: Let
me just jump in there

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:58.156
and say how easy it is
to change visualizations

00:27:58.156 --> 00:28:01.310
and to test particular
visualizations.

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:04.610
Geography is particularly
easy in Data Flow.

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:07.970
It just knows when you're
dealing with geographic data.

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:10.760
The way it handles
time based prompts

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:13.940
for you to be able to
choose a particular time

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:18.190
frame for whether you want
quarterly, monthly, trending,

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:19.010
et cetera.

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:21.410
It just sort of gets
all that, so the built

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:24.440
in sort of functionality
for constants like geography

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:27.180
and time is really pleasing.

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:31.070
If you look at on prem versions
of this kind of software,

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:34.220
it's not near that easy,
so Data Studio really

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:38.842
shines in handling those
sorts of known data types.

00:28:38.842 --> 00:28:39.800
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

