00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:17.990
So, questions of the week.

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:20.150
If you want to be
able to unit test

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:22.820
your integrations with
Kubernetes with client go.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:25.730
How can you mock what happens
inside the cluster in your unit

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:26.330
tests?

00:29:26.330 --> 00:29:26.830
Mark?

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:27.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
so this is something

00:29:27.860 --> 00:29:30.080
I do a lot with because I muck
around with Kubernetes a lot.

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:32.240
Probably if you're familiar
at all with Kubernetes,

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:33.980
you may be familiar
with Cube CTL.

00:29:33.980 --> 00:29:36.200
You're probably familiar
that from Cube CTL,

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:38.300
behind that is a Kubernetes API.

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:41.930
Client go is the go
client that you use for,

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:43.380
basically, talking
to Kubernetes.

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:44.900
So if you want to
programmatically create

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:46.733
pods or deployments,
or pretty much anything

00:29:46.733 --> 00:29:48.860
inside Kubernetes, can do
that through client go.

00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:51.380
What I don't know if is
as much known by people

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:53.930
is there's a whole
fake section to it that

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:57.410
enables you to basically mock
out how things work, so you

00:29:57.410 --> 00:29:58.869
create a client
set with client go,

00:29:58.869 --> 00:30:01.284
and then when you want to use
it in testing you can create

00:30:01.284 --> 00:30:03.410
this fake client set,
and what you can do then

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:05.420
is add these things
called reactors to it,

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:06.650
and I'll put links
in the show notes.

00:30:06.650 --> 00:30:08.540
Reactors are lovely in
that you can basically

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:10.750
control what happens when
things happen for the client's

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:10.900
[INAUDIBLE].

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:13.400
So you might be like, list
pods, and you'll add a list pods

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:14.360
reactor, and then
you'll be like,

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:16.382
these are the pods
that will be shown now,

00:30:16.382 --> 00:30:18.840
and it'll work just like it
would do as if it was connected

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:21.250
to a real cluster, but you
just have full control over it,

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:23.280
and you can basically tell it
to do whatever it is you need

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:24.480
to do inside your unit tests.

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:25.150
MELANIE WARRICK: That's nice.

00:30:25.150 --> 00:30:26.250
MARK MANDEL: It's
actually really nice.

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:27.280
MELANIE WARRICK:
Testing is hard enough,

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:29.330
so when you have something
that can help with that,

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:30.070
that's invaluable.

00:30:30.070 --> 00:30:30.920
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
so I'll put links

